nect our ideas  together and move from one which is  present to another which is absent  bringing them into you could say our  purview and these are all about what  Hume calls matters of effect so do these  he says provide belief he said he asks  does it happen in all these relations  that when one of the objects is  presented true the senses our memory the  mind is not only carried to the  conception of the correlative but  reaches a steadier and stronger  conception of it then what otherwise it  would have been able to attain steadier  and stronger conception is one of the  ways in which he has characterized the  feeling or sentiment of belief in the  truth or reality of that thing so he  says this seems to be the case with that  belief which arises from the relation of  cause and effect but can it be the same  with other relations or principles of  Association if so he says this may be  established as a general law which takes  place in all the operations of the mind  what would that mean not only that we  have belief going on but that we have  this force of customer habit taking  place in relation of resemblance and  contiguity as well as causation so this  is a very important transition and Hume  examines this by considering some  examples and we might come up with many  other examples on our own but I think  for the time being we should look at his  so he says our first experiment the  appearance of the picture of an absent  friend our idea of this person is  evidently enlivened by the resemblance  and that ver every passion which that  idea occasions whether of joy or sorrow  acquires new force and vigor in  producing this effect he says there  occur both a relation and a present  impression what's the present impression  looking at the picture and we don't  necessarily need to have you know like a  snapshot that we keep on our desk  although people do that right why why do  people keep pictures of their loved ones  on their desk or their dogs and cats  couch